<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      macOS packet-tunnel extension entitlements for Mac App Store.

      The extension is sandboxed too — on macOS an appex inherits the host's
      sandbox requirement, and NEProvider extensions are refused entirely if
      the sandbox entitlement is absent.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The tunnel's own outbound traffic. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      REQUIRED. Do not remove — removing it silently breaks every tunnel.

      Build 10421 dropped this key after App Review's AUTOMATED scan rejected
      10420 under guideline 2.4.5 for carrying it "without listener
      functionality". The scan was wrong, and the reasoning that accepted it
      (WireGuard is outbound-initiated, so replies ride the established UDP
      flow) confused the PROTOCOL with the SOCKET. Measured on macOS 26 with
      the shipping Mac App Store build:

          wg: Unable to update bind: listen udp4 :0: bind: operation not permitted
          wg: Interface state was Down, requested Up, now Down

      That is EPERM from the App Sandbox. wireguard-go opens its UDP socket
      with net.ListenUDP(":0") — a LISTENING bind, which the sandbox gates on
      network.server regardless of the fact that nothing unsolicited is ever
      accepted on it. Without this entitlement the interface never comes up, so
      not one packet is transmitted: no handshake, no traffic, and the UI shows
      "connected" over a dead tunnel because NEVPNStatus only reports that the
      extension started.

      Everything else was verified correct while chasing this: the peer was
      registered server-side with the right allowed IPs, and the client config
      carried the correct endpoint, server public key, routes and keepalive.
      The socket bind was the only thing failing.

      The HOST APP deliberately does NOT carry this key — it has no listener,
      so the scan's finding was fair there. It belongs on the extension alone.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      Same shared keychain group as the host app. The extension reads the
      WireGuard private key and preshared key from here; a mismatch fails with
      errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) and the tunnel dies at startTunnel with
      missingConfig — the exact failure already documented on the iOS side.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)app.birdo.vpn</string>
    </array>

</dict>
</plist>
